---
// The home hero: three text-only slots side by side, motion, placement and docking, left to right, split by two seams,
// then one shared app window under them (components/HeroWindow.astro) where all three libraries do their part. No title
// above the slots: the page's one h1 is the three headlines (its three spans, a real space between them). The TypeScript
// sentence is the first paragraph in the DOM and sits under the slots on screen.
import HeroWindow from "./HeroWindow.astro";

const arrow = '<svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3.5 8h9M9 4.5L12.5 8 9 11.5"/></svg>';
---
<section class="hero" aria-label="The three libraries">
  <p class="pair-intro">Three small TypeScript libraries for desktop-style web apps: one moves windows, panels and toolbars, one fits things into the room there is, and one docks panels and toolbars.</p>
  <div class="band" id="band">
    <div class="bg m" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="bg p" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="bg d" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="seam s1" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="seam s2" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <span class="eyebrow eb m">@n-uf/motion · how things move</span>
    <span class="eyebrow eb p">@n-uf/placement · where things fit</span>
    <span class="eyebrow eb d">@n-uf/docking · where panels and toolbars dock</span>
    <h1 class="pair"><span class="m" id="hm">Motion: movement that settles and never overshoots.</span> <span class="p" id="hp">Placement: the layout math for any interface.</span> <span class="d" id="hd">Docking: a ready model of an app window.</span></h1>
    <div class="col m">
      <p class="lead">Give it rects and the real elements glide there; a drag stays under the pointer.</p>
      <div class="install">
        <div class="cmdbar"><code class="cmd"><span class="prompt" aria-hidden="true">$</span> npm i @n-uf/motion</code><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="npm i @n-uf/motion" aria-label="Copy the install command for @n-uf/motion">Copy</button></div>
        <div class="go-links"><a class="btn primary" href="/window-motion/">See the motion rules<Fragment set:html={arrow} /></a><a class="btn quiet" href="/docs/motion/">Docs</a></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="col p">
      <p class="lead">Small functions you call where you need them: a popup that stays on screen beside its anchor, a row that shrinks in a set order, an area split into panes.</p>
      <div class="install">
        <div class="cmdbar p"><code class="cmd"><span class="prompt" aria-hidden="true">$</span> npm i @n-uf/placement</code><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="npm i @n-uf/placement" aria-label="Copy the install command for @n-uf/placement">Copy</button></div>
        <div class="go-links"><a class="btn primary p" href="/docs/placement/">Read the placement docs<Fragment set:html={arrow} /></a></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="col d">
      <p class="lead">Declare the places (bars, side columns, rails, floats) and what each takes. Docking keeps who sits where, folds panels in a set order as room runs out, answers every drag and saves the layout. You draw it.</p>
      <div class="install">
        <div class="cmdbar d"><code class="cmd"><span class="prompt" aria-hidden="true">$</span> npm i @n-uf/docking</code><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="npm i @n-uf/docking" aria-label="Copy the install command for @n-uf/docking">Copy</button></div>
        <div class="go-links"><a class="btn primary d" href="/docking-layouts/">See the docking layouts<Fragment set:html={arrow} /></a></div>
        <div class="go-links"><a class="btn quiet" href="/toolbar-groups/">Toolbar groups</a><a class="btn quiet" href="/docs/docking/">Docs</a></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <HeroWindow />
  <!-- The Copy buttons' announcement ("Copied"), read out politely (islands/copy.ts). -->
  <p class="sr-only" id="copy-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
</section>
